 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Decide if the relationship is direct (both increase) or inverse (one up, one down)",
   "Find the constant k: direct → k = y ÷ x, inverse → k = y × x",
   "Use k to find the missing value",
   "Check: does your answer make sense? (bigger or smaller?)"
  ],
  "title": "How to Solve Direct & Inverse Proportion",
  "content": "<p><strong>Direct proportion:</strong> as one quantity increases, the other increases at the same rate. If \\(y\\) is directly proportional to \\(x\\), then \\(y = kx\\) where \\(k\\) is the constant of proportionality.</p><p><strong>Inverse proportion:</strong> as one quantity increases, the other decreases. If \\(y\\) is inversely proportional to \\(x\\), then \\(y = \\frac{k}{x}\\).</p><p>The <strong>unitary method</strong> works for both: find the value for 1 unit, then scale. For inverse problems, multiply the two linked quantities to find \\(k\\), then divide \\(k\\) by the new value.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>5 identical books weigh 1.2 kg. What do 8 books weigh?</strong></p><p>Direct proportion. One book = \\(\\frac{1.2}{5} = 0.24\\) kg.</p><p>8 books = \\(8 \\times 0.24 = 1.92\\) kg.</p>"
 },
